---
import Layout from '../../layouts/Layout.astro';
import { sitePath } from '../../lib/siteUrl';
---

<Layout
  title="Print Production — Varve"
  description="Design with page-level bleed and trim guides in Varve, then carry the same print geometry into PDF/X export."
>
  <div class="container-custom">
    <div class="feature-page">
      <nav class="breadcrumb" aria-label="Breadcrumb">
        <a href={sitePath('/features')}>Features</a> / Print Production
      </nav>

      <h1>Print production you can see while designing</h1>
      <p class="feature-intro">
        Configure bleed on a page, inspect the production area on the canvas, and keep trim and
        export geometry tied to the same document settings.
      </p>

      <section class="feature-section">
        <h2>Bleed belongs to the page</h2>
        <p>
          A page's trim is its finished size. Bleed expands outward from that trim edge and is
          stored in the document's physical units. Page overrides inherit the document default,
          so mixed page sizes can keep their own production requirements without creating a
          second canvas-only setting.
        </p>
        <ul>
          <li>Set uniform or per-edge bleed in the Page Print inspector.</li>
          <li>Use millimetres, inches, points, or the document's supported unit.</li>
          <li>Keep bleed values separate from safe area, slug, and crop marks.</li>
        </ul>
      </section>

      <section class="feature-section">
        <h2>Trim and bleed guides on canvas</h2>
        <p>
          The dashed outer boundary and subtle production band are editor overlays, not artwork.
          Toggle them from View without changing the document, undo history, or exported files.
          Artwork that crosses the trim edge remains visible on the pasteboard, making missing
          background coverage easy to spot before export.
        </p>
      </section>

      <section class="feature-section">
        <h2>Print-aware export</h2>
        <p>
          PDF/X export expands the media geometry around trim and writes distinct MediaBox,
          BleedBox, TrimBox, CropBox, and ArtBox values. Crop marks reference trim, not the outer
          bleed edge. Raster and screen-PDF export paths remain format-specific; check the export
          capability before treating a bleed guide as an output promise.
        </p>
      </section>

      <section class="feature-section">
        <h2>Still in progress</h2>
        <p>
          Varve does not yet perform authoritative painted-coverage preflight for artwork that
          stops at trim. For press-critical work, leave the guides visible and inspect the final
          PDF/X proof. Multi-page PDF execution and full printer-mark preview remain separate
          roadmap items.
        </p>
      </section>

      <div class="feature-cta">
        <h2>Explore the complete export workflow</h2>
        <a href={sitePath('/features/export')} class="btn-primary">Export &amp; Code Generation</a>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>
</Layout>

<style>
  .feature-page { max-width: 800px; margin: 0 auto; padding: 3rem 0; }
  .breadcrumb { font-size: 0.875rem; margin-bottom: 1.5rem; }
  .breadcrumb a, .feature-section a { color: var(--text-secondary); }
  .feature-page h1 { font-size: clamp(2rem, 5vw, 2.5rem); font-family: var(--font-editorial); font-weight: 700; letter-spacing: -0.015em; margin-bottom: 1rem; color: var(--text-primary); }
  .feature-intro { font-size: 1.25rem; color: var(--text-secondary); margin-bottom: 3rem; }
  .feature-section { margin-bottom: 3rem; }
  .feature-section h2 { font-size: 1.5rem; font-weight: 700; margin-bottom: 1rem; color: var(--text-primary); }
  .feature-section p, .feature-section li { color: var(--text-secondary); line-height: 1.8; }
  .feature-section ul { padding-left: 1.25rem; }
  .feature-cta { text-align: center; padding: 3rem 0; border-top: 1px solid var(--border-default); }
  .feature-cta h2 { font-size: 1.25rem; margin-bottom: 1rem; color: var(--text-primary); }
</style>
